    "overall_outcome": "Seven compliance agents were reviewed for case TEST-2024-001 (LN-2024-001). No agent returned a clean Pass. HVCRE returned Not Applicable (Non-HVCRE classification, high confidence, no deficiencies beyond minor file notation gaps). FZD returned Compliant with Moderate confidence, subject to documentation gaps. HMDA, ECOA, HPML, YBS, and VMI all returned Potential Issue or Non-Compliant. Three cross-regime deficiency clusters were identified: (1) a rate spread and APR data contradiction affecting HMDA, ECOA, and HPML across all three regimes simultaneously; (2) a systemic demographic data and VMI documentation deficiency affecting HMDA and VMI and cross-cutting ECOA fair lending obligations; and (3) a systemic missing-evidence pattern affecting YBS, ECOA, HMDA, and HPML, centered on absent income, applicant identity, and co-borrower documentation. Additional isolated findings exist in HPML (appraisal delivery obligations, TRID Loan Estimate absence, flip-rule analysis gap) and FZD (flood determination metadata completeness, life-of-loan monitoring gap). The loan file in its current state is not examination-ready under FCA standards for any of the five regimes returning Potential Issue or Non-Compliant determinations.",
    "agents_reviewed": ["HMDA", "ECOA", "HPML", "HVCRE", "FZD", "YBS", "VMI"],
    "agents_missing": []
  },
  "cross_regime_findings": [
    {
      "root_cause": "Rate Spread and APR/APOR Data Contradiction",
      "affected_agents": ["HMDA", "ECOA", "HPML"],
      "impact_summary": "All three agents independently flagged the same irreconcilable discrepancy: the HMDA rate spread field reports 2.37 percentage points, while the structured APR (8.12%) and APOR (7.02%) fields produce a calculated spread of 1.10 percentage points. This single data error has cascading regulatory consequences across three regimes. For HMDA, the rate spread is a required LAR data field; an inaccurate value constitutes a material reporting deficiency subject to resubmission and FCA examination citation. For ECOA and HPML, the spread determines whether the loan is a first-lien Higher-Priced Mortgage Loan under Regulation Z (threshold: 1.50 pp). If the spread is 1.10 pp, HPML obligations do not attach; if 2.37 pp is correct, HPML status is confirmed, triggering mandatory escrow, appraisal independence, appraisal delivery, right-to-receive notice, ability-to-repay documentation, and flip-rule analysis requirements—all of which interact with ECOA fair lending and examination readiness obligations. The APOR source document (specific FFIEC weekly table entry for lock date 2024-04-10) and the APR calculation worksheet are absent from the file for all three agents, preventing resolution. The rate lock agreement is also absent, preventing independent verification of the correct APOR comparison date.",
      "severity": "High"
    },
    {
      "root_cause": "Demographic Data Not Extracted or Recorded Despite Confirmed VMI Collection",
      "affected_agents": ["HMDA", "VMI", "ECOA"],
      "impact_summary": "HMDA, VMI, and ECOA each independently identified that the demographic information block (ethnicity, race, sex) is entirely empty for both the primary applicant (John Smith) and the co-applicant (Mary Smith), despite vmi_presented_to_applicant and applicant_provided_vmi both being flagged true. This creates an internal data inconsistency: process flags indicate collection occurred, but no collected values exist in any record field. This single root cause simultaneously produces: a HMDA LAR reporting deficiency (12 CFR 1003.4(a)(10)(i)-(ii) requires ethnicity, race, and sex for both applicants); a Regulation B / 12 CFR 1002.13 record-keeping deficiency (collected data must be retained); a VMI documentation deficiency (no form identifier, no collection date, no co-applicant record, no refusal documentation, no applicant acknowledgment of voluntary nature); and a fair lending examination gap under ECOA (consistency of treatment analysis requires demographic records). Additionally, disaggregated ethnicity and race sub-categories required by the 2018 HMDA rule are absent. The firewall and segregation controls are asserted by boolean flags only, without corroborating audit logs or access reports, which all three agents flagged as insufficient for examination purposes.",
      "severity": "High"
    },
    {
      "root_cause": "Systemic Missing Income, Identity, and Co-Borrower Documentation Across Regimes",
      "affected_agents": ["HMDA", "ECOA", "HPML", "YBS", "VMI"],
      "impact_summary": "Five agents independently identified overlapping gaps in borrower income documentation, applicant identity documentation, and co-borrower (Mary Smith) data. HMDA requires gross annual income relied on in the credit decision for LAR reporting (12 CFR 1003.4(a)(10)(iii)); the income field is absent. HPML requires documented income verification for ability-to-repay under 12 CFR 1026.43 for a first-lien HPML; income verification documents (tax returns, Schedule F, farm financial statements) are not confirmed in the file. YBS requires gross agricultural sales documentation via Schedule F or tax returns to confirm the Small Farmer threshold, and requires government-issued ID or birth certificate for both borrowers to confirm Young Farmer age eligibility. ECOA notes the absence of individual credit reports for each joint applicant, preventing independent creditworthiness verification and fair lending consistency review. VMI notes the complete absence of co-applicant (Mary Smith) data of any kind, including age, farming experience, demographic information, and YBS eligibility, despite the joint application. This pattern reflects a systemic documentation standard deficiency: the file was assembled with primary closing instruments but lacks the underlying borrower-evidence layer required for multi-regime regulatory examination readiness. The shared root cause is the absence of a file assembly standard that requires retention of income verification, identity, and co-borrower documentation as a baseline for all covered transactions.",
      "severity": "High"
    },
    {
